## 3. Key Practical Use Cases

### Use Case 1: Preserving Correct Prototype Getters (`receiver` parameter)

The `receiver` parameter in `Reflect.get(target, prop, receiver)` ensures that if a property is a getter on a prototype, `this` inside the getter points to the proxy instance rather than the raw underlying target object.

```javascript
const parent = {
  name: "Parent",
  get greeting() {
    return `Hello, I am ${this.name}`;
  }
};

const child = Object.create(parent);
child.name = "Child";

const proxy = new Proxy(child, {
  get(target, prop, receiver) {
    // Passing 'receiver' ensures `this` inside parent.greeting points to 'child' (proxy)
    return Reflect.get(target, prop, receiver);
  }
});

console.log(proxy.greeting); // "Hello, I am Child" (Without receiver, it would return "Hello, I am Parent")

```

---

### Use Case 2: Clean `Proxy` Forwarding

`Reflect` makes writing transparent or custom Proxy traps concise and error-free:

```javascript
const user = { firstName: "Jane", lastName: "Doe" };

const autoLoggerProxy = new Proxy(user, {
  get(target, prop, receiver) {
    console.log(`[READ ACCESS]: Reading property '${String(prop)}'`);
    return Reflect.get(target, prop, receiver);
  },
  
  set(target, prop, value, receiver) {
    console.log(`[WRITE ACCESS]: Setting property '${String(prop)}' = ${value}`);
    return Reflect.set(target, prop, value, receiver); // Returns boolean automatically
  }
});

autoLoggerProxy.firstName;          // Logs: [READ ACCESS]: Reading property 'firstName'
autoLoggerProxy.lastName = "Smith"; // Logs: [WRITE ACCESS]: Setting property 'lastName' = Smith

```

---

### Use Case 3: Flexible Constructor Instantiation (`Reflect.construct`)

`Reflect.construct` allows you to instantiate an object using an array of dynamic arguments, or override the `new.target` prototype constructor.

```javascript
class Person {
  constructor(name, age) {
    this.name = name;
    this.age = age;
  }
}

// Dynamically pass arguments as an array (similar to Function.prototype.apply for constructors)
const args = ["Charlie", 28];
const person = Reflect.construct(Person, args);

console.log(person.name); // "Charlie"

```

---

## `Object` vs `Reflect` Comparison

| Feature                 | `Object` Static Methods                                             | `Reflect` Static Methods                       |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| **Failure Handling**    | Often throws `TypeError` on failure.                                | Returns `false` on failure (no thrown error).  |
| **Primitive Arguments** | Coerces primitives (e.g. `Object.keys('abc')` → `['0', '1', '2']`). | Throws `TypeError` if target is not an object. |
| **Return Values**       | Returns the modified object (e.g. `Object.defineProperty`).         | Returns `boolean` status indicator.            |
| **Proxy Traps**         | No 1-to-1 match.                                                    | Exact 1-to-1 match with all Proxy traps.       |
